/* =====================================================================
   LAYER 1 — THE STORM DRAINS
   The top of the sewer band, just under the street: the CLIMAX layer, where
   the escape happens. Register: rain, grates, daylight, the smell of the
   surface — and the betrayal of all of it. The filth here is at its most
   ordinary (chemical street-runoff, foam, dead rats), which is exactly what
   makes it land: you can see the clean world through the grates and you can't
   reach it, and the runoff tells you the deep is already leaking UP into the
   city you're trying to get back to.

   Hub = TheCatchbasin (enter from L2 via TheRisers). The escape spine:
   Outfall (find the honest route → $foundRealExit) → Maintenance (the GATE:
   needs the rope) → ExitShaft (the climb → the corruption-keyed endings).
   Depths registered in setup.depthOf (= 1). Built as a full ~20-room layer.
   ===================================================================== */


/* ---------- HUB (enter from L2 TheRisers) ---------- */

:: TheCatchbasin [upper]
<<nobr>>
The risers let you out into the storm drains, and the storm drains are a different
country from the trunk mains below: built for rain, not waste, generous and grey
and almost clean, and laced all over their ceiling with grates and gulley-mouths
that leak the one thing you have not seen in what feels like a year. Daylight. Thin
and grey and falling in dusty bars through a dozen openings onto the catch-basin's
standing water, and with it the smell of rain, and far off the drowned grumble of
traffic. The surface. Right there. A body's length of brick and iron away.
<</nobr>>

<<if $corruption gte 70>>
And the light is *wrong* on you now — too thin, too cold, too honest, picking out
the black in the seams of your hands and asking questions you have stopped being
able to answer. You notice you have moved out of the bars of daylight without
deciding to, into the good clean shadow, and that the noticing comes a half-beat
too late to do anything about.
<<elseif $corruption gte 35>>
The daylight ought to feel like hope and mostly it does, but there's a new thing
under the hope now, a small resentment of the brightness, a sense that the clean
grey world up there has no idea what it costs to climb back into it and would not
thank you for the telling.
<<else>>
You could weep. You don't, because weeping is a thing you have been saving, but you
stand in the falling light with your face turned up to it like a plant, and breathe
rain, and let yourself believe for one whole minute that this ends with you up there.
<</if>>

The drains run off every way, all of them tending, you can feel it, toward the
light.

<<link "A chamber with a real draft — the way out, maybe">><<goto "Outfall">><</link>>
<<link "A maintenance shaft, with a chain of daylight at the top">><<goto "Maintenance">><</link>>
<<link "A dry gauge-house, off the basin">><<goto "TheGaugeHouse">><</link>>
<<link "A street-catch, choked with what the gutters swallowed">><<goto "TheStreetCatch">><</link>>
<<link "A great overflow chamber, to the west">><<goto "TheStormFront">><</link>>
<<link "A long gallery under a street, grates all along its roof">><<goto "TheGalleryOfGrates">><</link>>
<<link "Back down the risers, toward the trunk mains">><<goto "TheRisers">><</link>>


/* ---------- THE ESCAPE SPINE (moved from core; re-linked into L1) ---------- */

:: Outfall [upper]
A real draft pours from a collapsed culvert here, cold and mineral and almost
clean, and following your nose toward it is the first thing that has felt like
hope since the sack came down. The passage narrows to a throat of cracked
concrete, then opens onto a chamber where grey light leaks down through a storm
grating far overhead. You can see *sky* through it, a coin of dirty daylight.

This is the honest way out. There is a maintenance shaft off this chamber with
iron rungs set into the wall — except a whole stretch of them, above head
height, has rusted clean away, leaving a smooth gap of wet brick between you and
the light. You'd need something to bridge it. A rope. A chain. Something.

You fix the route in your mind: left at the sump, past the grit chamber, here.

<<set $foundRealExit to true>>

<<link "To the maintenance shaft">><<goto "Maintenance">><</link>>
<<link "Back to the catch-basin">><<goto "TheCatchbasin">><</link>>


:: Maintenance [upper]
The maintenance shaft is the most ordinary thing in this whole place and you
could weep at the sight of it: a brick chimney, a chain of grey daylight at the
top, the smell of rain thinning the reek. This is the seam between down-here and
up-there.

Step in under it and the shaft resolves out of the dimness — narrower than the
light let you hope, a man's-width flue of old engineering brick gone slick and
green. A council number is stencilled at chest height, half eaten by rust-bloom,
the last honest mark anyone made down here before the dark had the place to
itself.

The rain comes down the flue in a fine constant drizzle, ringing faint on the
brick, beading on your upturned face, and for all its ordinariness the shaft has
the held quiet of a thing that has watched a great many people stand exactly
here and look exactly up.

<<if $sealed>>
You set your hand to the cold brick to start the climb, and there is no climb.
The shaft is gone — weeping wall where the daylight was — and rising in you is the
unhurried, final certainty that there was never a way up from here, that you have
always been down here, that the grey coin of sky you remember was a thing you
dreamed to keep your legs moving.
<<elseif not $foundRealExit>>
But you haven't yet found the honest route to it through the maze — you're not
even sure this is the right shaft. There must be a way to be certain.
<<elseif not setup.has("rope")>>
But the rungs above head height are gone — a smooth gap of rusted brick between
you and the rest of the climb. You can't free-climb wet brick. You need a rope
or a chain to bridge it, and the only place that sells such things is back down
through the teeth, in the market.
<<else>>
You uncoil the rope, swing the weighted end up over the lowest sound rung above
the gap, and it catches on the second throw. The bridge is made. All that's left
is to climb it — and to decide what you're climbing out *as*.
<</if>>

<<if $sealed>>
<<link "Stop. Stop hoping.">><<goto "EndingTrapped">><</link>>
<<else>>
<<if $foundRealExit and setup.has("rope")>>
<<link "Climb out">><<goto "ExitShaft">><</link>>
<</if>>
<<link "Back to the chamber with the draft">><<goto "Outfall">><</link>>
<<link "Back to the catch-basin">><<goto "TheCatchbasin">><</link>>
<</if>>


:: ExitShaft [upper]
You set your hands on the rope. The iron above the gap is cold — actually cold,
the cold of the upper world — and the rain coming down the shaft tastes of a city
that has no idea what lives under it. Above you, the coin of grey light. Behind
you, the dark leans in close, curious whether you'll really go.

The rope creaks as you test your weight on it, the weighted end snug over the
sound rung, the bridge holding. Above the gap the rungs resume, true and climbable
all the way to the slot of day, each one nearer the noise of the surface — gulls,
a far horn, the long hiss of tyres on wet road, the whole indifferent racket of
being alive up there, getting louder the higher you let your eye go.

And you feel it now, the thing the climb is really asking, settling into your
arms before they've taken the first pull: that going up is the easy part, that the
gap was never only in the wall, and that whatever sets its hands on this rope will
arrive at the top, and the only open question down here in the cold is what.

<<link "Climb out">>
  <<if $sealed>>
    <<goto "EndingTrapped">>
  <<elseif $corruption gte 70>>
    <<goto "EndingPyrrhic">>
  <<else>>
    <<goto "EndingTrueEscape">>
  <</if>>
<</link>>
<<link "Not yet — back to the catch-basin">><<goto "TheCatchbasin">><</link>>


/* ---------- REST ---------- */

:: TheGaugeHouse [upper]
Off the catch-basin there is a gauge-house — a dry brick cell where the city once
kept its instruments to measure the storm-water, to know its own floods. The gear
is long dead, the dial-glasses crazed, the paper charts furred to felt, but the
roof holds and the floor is dry and a high slot of a window lets in a clean blade
of daylight that falls across a bench worn smooth by the backs of men who sat here
out of the rain, a hundred years ago, doing an honest and forgotten job.

Closer to, the cell gives up its small archaeology. A tin mug, gone to a single
rust-coloured stain on the bench. A coat-hook with a green shred of someone's coat
still on it. A tide of pencilled tallies up one doorpost — rainfalls counted, shifts
served, the patient bookkeeping of men who measured the water and went home to dry
beds. Nobody died in here. That, more than the dry floor, is what makes it feel safe:
this was a room where the worst that happened was weather.

You sit where they sat. The light is real and the quiet is real and for the length
of a held breath you are just a person out of the weather, waiting for a shift to
end. The shaking settles. The small surface part of you, the part that still
remembers being expected somewhere, sits up a little.
<<if $hp lt $maxhp>><<run setup.passTime(setup.REST_TIME)>><</if>><<set $hp to $maxhp>>

<<link "A utility vault, through the back hatch">><<goto "TheJunctionVault">><</link>>
<<link "Back to the catch-basin">><<goto "TheCatchbasin">><</link>>


/* ---------- CACHES / PUZZLE ---------- */

:: TheStreetCatch [upper]
A street-catch — the sump under a kerbside gulley, where everything the gutters
swallow comes to rest before the storm-water carries it on: a sediment of the
ordinary world, grey and sodden and heartbreaking. Bottle-caps. A child's barrette.
Keys. The swollen ghost of a paperback. And coins, of course, the city's small
change, washed down out of the bright economy overhead into this dark one.

Overhead the gulley-mouth makes a small skylight of the kerb, and through the bars
of it the surface comes down in pieces — a wash of grit, a sweet-wrapper turning
slowly on the standing water, the smell of wet pavement and somebody's dropped
chips going to rot. You stand in the soft column of it. The catch is no bigger than
a coffin and shaped a little like one, and you have the strange sense of looking up
out of a grave that the city is, with no malice, busily filling.

The sediment goes down further than your hand will reach, layer on patient layer,
the whole strata of small lost things compacting toward the bottom — and that, you
understand, is the deal the surface offers everything that slips its grip: not the
river, not the sea, just this, a slow grey settling in the dark under the kerb,
worth less every year, never quite gone.

<<if $streetCatchLooted>>
You've been back to pick the spendable from the sad, and left the rest — the keys to
doors you will never stand at, the barrette, the book — to go on being the surface's
quiet sediment in the dark.
<<else>>
There's coin among the sediment, the city's small change washed down out of the bright
economy overhead into this dark one — the kind of find that only really pulls you back
when the thought of it catches you somewhere else along the drains.
<</if>>

<<link "Along a silt-gulley">><<goto "TheGulley">><</link>>
<<link "Back to the catch-basin">><<goto "TheCatchbasin">><</link>>


:: TheGulley [upper]
A long silt-gulley runs off the catch, shin-deep in the soft grey paste the storm-
water drops when it slows: a slick of the city's sheddings, cigarette-ends and
grit and the mica-glitter of a million ground-down things, and here and there the
duller glint of something that was worth more before the gutter had it.

Each step in is a small theft of effort — the paste closes over your foot with a
slow suck and gives it back grudging, and the wading sets the surface of the silt
to shifting, a sluggish grey weather that releases as it stirs the flat sweet
chemical smell of everything dissolved and resettled, road-tar and detergent and
the ghost of a thousand drains. The walls run close and low. The light from the
catch behind you thins to nothing a dozen paces in.

It is very quiet down the gulley, the quiet of a place where the water has stopped
hurrying and the city has stopped reaching, and the silt lies in long undisturbed
drifts that show, when you read them, the slow set of the slowing flow — and the
soft suggestion, the longer you stand still in it, that you are reading your own
forecast, that this is simply where things go when at last they no longer have the
current to keep moving.

<<if $gulleyLooted>>
You've combed it. What's left is only silt, and the patient suggestion that you,
too, are something the slowing water could set down and leave.
<<else>>
Here and there in the soft grey paste is the duller glint of something that was worth
more before the gutter had it — worth going back for, if you find yourself thinking of
it from somewhere up the line.
<</if>>

<<link "Toward a culvert running under a street">><<goto "TheCulvertRun">><</link>>
<<link "Back to the street-catch">><<goto "TheStreetCatch">><</link>>


:: TheStrainer [upper]
A trash-screen bars the channel here — a rake of iron bars set across the flow to
comb out the big debris before it reaches the river, and a century of combings have
built against it a sodden wall of branch and bottle and bin-bag and worse, dammed
so tight the storm-water has worried a new way around through a side-cut. Wedged in
the trash, just out of reach, are things the current was carrying that someone
might once have wanted back.

Up close the wall is a cross-section, a core sample driven through the city's whole
recent history: down at the waterline it is black and slick and anaerobic, gone to a
peat that is barely things any more, and it lightens as it climbs, through compacted
strata of carrier-bag and cigarette-packet and the bleached labels of brands that
no longer exist, to a fresh untidy crust of this season's litter at the top, still
bright, still recognisable, not yet taught what the rest below it has learned.

The flow finds its way around through the side-cut with a thin continuous trickle,
the only moving thing in the chamber, and the screen stands against the dammed
weight of decades without complaint — a thing built to stop the world's refuse going
out to the river, doing its one job so faithfully it has become the very wall it was
set to drain.

<<if $strainerLooted>>
You've taken what was worth the reaching. The screen holds its grey wall of refuse,
patient, adding to itself a little with every rain, the way everything down here
adds to itself a little with every rain.
<<else>>
Wedged in the trash, just out of reach, are things the current was carrying that
someone might once have wanted back — there for whoever's willing to come back and
work an arm into the screen's wall to the shoulder for them.
<</if>>

<<link "A barred outfall, toward the river">><<goto "TheRiverMouth">><</link>>
<<link "Back to the overflow chamber">><<goto "TheStormFront">><</link>>


/* ---------- SNARES (bad ends) ---------- */

:: TheAreaGrate [upper]
At the gallery's end the roof drops to a wide pavement-grate, close enough to
touch, and through it — the *street*. Real pavement, real rain falling past real
streetlight, the underside of the ordinary world an arm's length up. And feet:
people, passing, their footsteps ringing on the iron, going about the bright cheap
business of being alive and above. You could call out. You are already calling out.

You press up to the grate and the world pours through it onto your upturned face —
cold rain, the white flutter of a passing umbrella, exhaust and wet stone and the
sugared warmth of a coffee somebody is carrying somewhere with somewhere to be. The
bars are slick under your fingers and close-spaced, a hand's width each, the gap
between you and the street the width of a wrist and the whole patient weight of the
city. You can see legs. You can see the hems of coats. You shout, and the sound goes
up into the rain and the traffic and the great indifference and is not, by anyone,
heard.

A pair of feet stops. Slowly, with a care that should reassure you and does the
opposite, someone crouches down to the grate, and a face comes into the light to
look at you — a kind face, a helping face, a face that says //there you are, hold
on, I've got you// in a warm and ordinary voice. And you have spent so long down
here learning what wears a face like that, and how a half-beat late the eyes come
to the smile, and you understand, one breath too slow, that the deep does not only
keep what falls into it. Some of it climbs out. Some of it got out *before* you, and
is up there now, wearing the city, and it has been waiting by the grates for one of
its own to come calling.

<<link "Reach up. Take the helping hand.">><<goto "EndingHelper">><</link>>
<<link "Don't. Get back from the grate, into the dark.">>
  <<soothe 4>>
  <<goto "TheGalleryOfGrates">>
<</link>>


:: TheFoamfall [upper]
Where a side-channel drops into the overflow, the falling water beats itself into
foam — a great slow heap of it, blue-white and faintly luminous, piling and
trembling and breaking apart, exactly the clean bright foam of a weir in a park, of
soap, of a bath. It smells of nothing, which after everything is its own kind of
miracle, and the urge to push your face into it, to let it scour the deep off your
skin, to be for one moment a clean thing in clean foam, is very strong.

Come nearer and the heap is bigger than it looked from the doorway — chest-high
against the channel wall, a whole drift of it, lit faintly from within where the
thin dark water shows through the lather, and it moves the way a living thing
breathes, swelling and subsiding, sloughing off islands that sail a slow yard and
dissolve. The roar of the fall behind it is huge and close and white. Spray comes
off it onto your face, cool and weightless, and where it lands it does not feel like
filth. It feels like the cleanest thing for a mile down.

That is the trick of it, and some old animal under your hunger to be clean knows it
before your mind will: nothing this far down is clean, and the brighter and softer
and more like a bath a thing looks here, the harder you should ask what it is made
of and what the city beat it up out of to make it look like that. The foam goes on
breathing, patient, faintly glowing, asking nothing, offering everything.

<<linkreplace "Wash your face in the clean white foam">>
<<corrupt "head" "filth" 26>>
You plunge your face into it and it is cold and soft and it does not smell of soap
at all once you are inside it — it smells of nothing because it has nothing left to
smell *with*, it is the froth the chemistry beats up out of the runoff, the surface-
tension skin of everything the city dissolved and flushed, and it goes into your
eyes and your open mouth and the soft tissues of you with a chemical eagerness, and
where it touches it takes — not the filth, the filth it leaves, but the thin live
membrane under the filth, the part of you that was still, technically, clean.
<</linkreplace>>

<<link "Pull back from the foam, eyes streaming">>
  <<soothe 2>>
  <<goto "TheStormFront">>
<</link>>


/* ---------- COMBAT ---------- */

:: TheRatDrift [upper]
The culvert widens into a slack-water bay where the storm-drift fetches up, and the
drift here is rats — a sodden grey raft of them, drowned in the last storm and
washed to this dead corner to rot, hundreds of them matted into one slow-turning
mass against the brick. The smell is enormous and almost ordinary, almost honest,
the clean rot of real dead animals, and it is nearly a comfort until the raft
*moves*.

<<if $ratDriftCleared>>
The drift lies still now, its one living tenant dealt with, the drowned and the
dead-again turning slowly together in the slack water, going wherever the next storm
decides to take them.

<<link "On, toward the rain-shaft">><<goto "TheRainShaft">><</link>>
<<link "Back along the culvert">><<goto "TheCulvertRun">><</link>>
<<else>>
One rat in the raft is not drowned. It has been *living* on the drowned, fattening
in the dark on a hundred of its dead kin, and it comes off the raft swollen and
wrong and far too large, hauling itself toward the only warm thing in the bay, which
is you.

<<scentof "rat">>
<<link "Put the bloated thing down">>
  <<run setup.startFight("rat", "TheRatDriftCleared")>>
  <<goto "Combat">>
<</link>>
<<link "Back along the culvert, away from the drift">><<goto "TheCulvertRun">><</link>>
<</if>>


:: TheRatDriftCleared [upper]
/* lands here after the rat-drift fight; sets the flag, then folds onward */
<<set $ratDriftCleared to true>>
You put it down, and it falls back among its dead, and the whole raft settles with a
soft wet sigh, as if grateful to be only dead again. You wipe your hands on the
brick. There is a rain-shaft beyond the bay, the clean fall of weather coming down
out of the world.

<<link "On, toward the rain-shaft">><<goto "TheRainShaft">><</link>>
<<link "Back along the culvert">><<goto "TheCulvertRun">><</link>>


/* ---------- PASSAGE / ATMOSPHERE FILLER ---------- */

:: TheStormFront [upper]
The great overflow chamber, built to swallow the storm-surge so the streets above
stay dry: a vault the size of a church, dry now and echoing, its floor stepped down
in wide terraces to a central channel, every surface stained to a high-water mark
far over your head that tells you what this room is for and what it becomes when the
sky opens.

Your footsteps go out across the dry floor and come back changed, doubled and
hollowed by the size of the place, and as your eyes climb the chamber it keeps
getting bigger — the terraces stepping down and away, the ceiling lost in dimness,
the channel at the bottom a black slot running out under the far wall to wherever
the water goes. The high-water mark rings the whole vault at a height that makes no
sense until you make yourself believe it: this entire cathedral of a room fills,
and has filled, and the stain is just where it last stopped.

There is weather coming. You can smell it — a pressure, a green edge on the air, the
charged stillness that comes before the sky lets go — and some animal part of you
reads the high-water mark and wants very much to not be standing in this room when
the city decides to flush. The dry quiet has a held-breath quality to it. The vault
is waiting for rain, the way it has waited a hundred years, and you are standing
where the rain will be.

<<link "A trash-screen, barring the channel">><<goto "TheStrainer">><</link>>
<<link "A fall of clean white foam, off the side">><<goto "TheFoamfall">><</link>>
<<link "Toward a clanking tide-gate">><<goto "TheTideFlap">><</link>>
<<link "Back to the catch-basin">><<goto "TheCatchbasin">><</link>>


:: TheRiverMouth [upper]
The drain ends, or means to: a great round outfall opening onto the river, the city's
old apology to the water, the place all of this was always headed. And it is *open* —
no brick here, no seal, just a screen of heavy bars set across the mouth to keep the
big debris in, and beyond them the river, grey and wide and moving, and beyond the
river the far bank with its lights, the whole indifferent breathing city laid out
in the rain. You get your hands on the bars. They do not give. They were set to hold
back a flood of refuse; a single person is, to them, just more refuse, held.

The bars are wrist-thick and set close, furred with the river's green where the
tide reaches and bright-worn where it doesn't, and the gaps between them are not even
the width of a hopeful thought. Real wind comes through — proper river wind, salt
and diesel and cold open distance — and it moves your hair and dries your eyes and
carries the slap of wake against the outfall stones, the whole living sound of the
estuary going about its enormous business a forearm's length beyond the iron.

You stand with your face to the bars and the cold river air on you and the city
right there across the water, and learn the particular cruelty of the storm drains:
that the way out is everywhere, and barred everywhere, and that the surface keeps
you out with exactly the same care it once took to keep its waste in.

<<if not $barHolderHeard>>
There is someone else at the bars. A woman — or the long-held idea of one — both hands locked on
the iron exactly where yours are, worn so far into the screen that the river has made her half its
own. She is not looking at the river. She has finished looking at the river.
<<linkreplace "Speak to the shape with both hands on the bars">><<nobr>><<set $barHolderHeard to true>>
//I had both hands on it, love — just here, where you've got yours,// she says, not turning. //I
thought I'd hold till someone up top looked down and saw a person and not a sack the river fetched
up. I lost the count of winters in it.// Her knuckles do not loosen; they have forgotten how. //You
stop being someone who's holding on. You become a thing the screen holds. And there's a mercy in it,
when you let it come — you were never going to be let out, so you can put down the dreadful work of
deserving to be.// A long quiet, the river going by. //Loosen one finger. Go on. See how it rests
you.//
<</nobr>><<corrupt "head" "filth" 3>><</linkreplace>>
<<else>>
She has gone back to her holding-on, or to her having-let-go; at this distance you can no longer
tell which, and neither, you suspect, can she.
<</if>>

<<link "Back to the trash-screen">><<goto "TheStrainer">><</link>>
<<link "Toward a clanking tide-gate">><<goto "TheTideFlap">><</link>>


:: TheSubwayBreach [upper]
The drain's wall has failed here against something older and squarer — a tunnel of
dressed concrete and dead cable, a transit tunnel, the underground proper, breached
where the storm-water ate through and the two undergrounds met. It is flooded
black and silent, the rails gone to rust under the water, but every so often a
pressure rolls down it and a far light swells and fades and the whole flooded tube
*breathes*, and you understand a train is still running somewhere on the live part
of the line, full of people in the dry lit world, a wall's thickness and an entire
universe away.

Through the ragged mouth of the breach the tunnel opens up wrong and grand — a
proper bore, tiled once, a strip of dead cable looping along the wall like a
dropped necklace, a platform edge sliding away into the flood-black with the white
stripe still painted along its lip. MIND THE GAP, the worn floor still tells the
drowned. A signboard hangs from one chain over the still water, its lettering gone,
pointing the way to a WAY OUT that the water closed a long time ago.

The flooded part holds the cold like a held breath, and the still black surface
gives back the breach behind you, and you, leaning into it. When the pressure rolls
down and the far light swells, the whole drowned mirror of it lifts toward gold and
then lets go, and for the second the light lasts you can see clean down the tunnel
to where the dry world keeps running — close enough to time, far enough that timing
it is the cruelest game the drains have taught you yet.

<<if not $breachKnown>>
Something stands at the breach, pressed to the gap where the storm-drain spills into the flooded
tube — shaped like a person waiting on a platform and turned the wrong way by the runoff that made
it. It is watching the dark water for the trains. It has the timetable nearly learned.
<<linkreplace "Ask it what it's waiting for">><<nobr>><<set $breachKnown to true>>
//Up,// it says, without turning, in a voice like a far train. //Everything down here is going up,
given long enough — the water taught me that. You think the breach is where the clean tunnel got
//in.// It's where //we// get out. Up the breaches and the gulleys and the grates, into the lit dry
world, to wear it.// A pressure rolls down the tube; the far light swells and dies. //I'll have the
gap between two trains timed to the second, soon. Then I ride one up in someone's coat, and no one
aboard reads me right until it's far too late to matter — the same way,// it says, almost kindly,
//no one read you.//
<</nobr>><<corrupt "head" "filth" 9>><</linkreplace>>
<<else>>
It has stopped noticing you; it is counting the dark between trains, and the count is all there is
of it now. A pressure builds in the tube, and the far light begins, again, to swell.
<</if>>

<<link "Toward a rain-shaft">><<goto "TheRainShaft">><</link>>
<<link "Back to the utility vault">><<goto "TheJunctionVault">><</link>>


:: TheCulvertRun [upper]
A long low culvert carries you under what must be a street — you can hear it
overhead, the grind and hiss of traffic, the rhythm of a city that has somewhere to
be — and the run is straight and grey and almost clean, walked once a year by a man
with a torch and a clipboard who is not due for eleven months. Painted on the wall
at intervals, fading, the cheerful institutional code of the surface: a number, an
arrow, a date long past, the breadcrumbs of people who came down here on purpose and
went home after.

You have to stoop, and the stooping makes the run feel longer than it is, the grey
walls sliding past close on both sides, the floor's worn channel guiding your feet.
Every so often a gulley-mouth in the ceiling lets down a shaft of weak day and a
patter of street-grit, and you pass through the falling light and out the other side,
back into the dim, and the traffic-grind overhead rises and ebbs as the road above
crosses and recrosses your unseen path. It is the most ordinary stretch of the whole
descent. That is almost what makes it frightening — how easily a thing could walk
here once a year and never know what shares it.

Off a side-bay, under the smell of wet brick and old water, there is another smell —
warm, animal, fermented, a reek with a *hunger* in it that your stomach answers before
your nose has finished deciding whether it's foul.

<<link "Toward a slack-water bay, full of drift">><<goto "TheRatDrift">><</link>>
<<link "Toward the warm, hungry reek in the side-bay">><<goto "TheLarder">><</link>>
<<link "Along a silt-gulley">><<goto "TheGulley">><</link>>
<<link "On toward a rain-shaft">><<goto "TheRainShaft">><</link>>


:: TheLarder [upper]
The side-bay is half-blocked by it: a delivery pallet, shrink-wrap burst, tumbled
down some loading-dock drain in a storm and fetched up here in the dark — and stacked
on it, sweating in the damp, a whole consignment of cheese. Good cheese, once. Stinky
cheese, the expensive kind, the washed-rind and the blue-veined and the soft ripe
rounds gone well past ripe into something that is no longer quite food and not yet
quite rot, the two states holding hands. Who knows why a pallet of stinky cheeses
ended up down in the sewer. Your belly rumbles as you look at it, loud and frank and
not remotely on your side.

And the rats are here. Of course the rats are here — fat and unbothered and many,
nesting in the burst rounds, grooming each other on the warm reeking wax, looking at
you not with the usual scurrying fear but with a kind of patient collegial interest,
as if you have arrived, at last, for a job they have been keeping open.

<<if setup.cheeseCompelled()>>
You are not going to manage not to. You know it stepping into the bay — your hands
know it, your jaw knows it, the rat that most of you now is knows it — and the small
human remainder of you watches from somewhere behind your own eyes as your body
crosses to the pallet and breaks off a fistful of the reeking soft and eats, fast,
crouched, among the rats, who shift to make room the way you make room for family.
<<set _r to setup.eatCheese()>>
<<nobr>>
//(the body feeds itself: +<<= _r.heal>> vigor, and the rat works <<= _r.bite>> deeper into you<<if _r.mana gt 0>>; the Word wells up +<<= _r.mana>> mana<</if>>)//
<</nobr>>
<<elseif not setup.inuredEnough()>>
Your belly howls at the sight of it, loud and frank and not on your side — but you cannot. You
stand over the pallet and your body will not do the thing the hunger is screaming for: it will not
put //that// in your mouth, the reeking soft, the rot holding hands with itself; it will not lie
down in //that// to be groomed into something new. The fresh animal you still are keeps its one
last refusal. The rats watch you fail to, unbothered, patient, with the look of things that have
seen this exact hesitation a hundred times and know exactly how it ends.
<<else>>
<<if $cheeseTasted>>
You told yourself, last time, that it was the once. Your mouth is already wet.
<</if>>

<<linkreplace "Eat — your belly has already decided">><<nobr>>
<<set _r to setup.eatCheese()>>
You break off a piece of the soft reeking ripe and eat it, and it is — god help you —
//good//, rich and animal and exactly what some new part of you has been wanting, and
it sits warm in you and the warmth has teeth. The rats watch you eat with what is
unmistakably approval.
//(+<<= _r.heal>> vigor, <<= _r.bite>> rat worked into you<<if _r.mana gt 0>>; +<<= _r.mana>> mana<</if>>)//
<</nobr>><</linkreplace>>
<</if>>

<<if setup.inuredEnough()>>
<<linkreplace "Bed down in the nest among the pallet — just to rest">><<nobr>>
<<set _r to setup.ratNestRest()>>
You make a hollow for yourself in the warm reeking heap and the rats do not object;
they close in around you instead, a living blanket, breathing and twitching and
warm, and you sleep the best sleep you have had down here — and wake rested to the
back teeth and groomed, washed all over by a hundred small tongues, smelling of the
nest, of them, of what you are evidently becoming while you sleep.
//(rested to full — and <<= _r.bite>> rat groomed into you<<if _r.mana gt 0>>; the Word in you full again<</if>>)//
<</nobr>><</linkreplace>>
<</if>>



Deeper in the nest, where the heaped wax is oldest and the dark is warmest, something sits
that was a man further along than you are: a rat-thing gone almost all the way over, hunched
and grey and patient, whiskered, its hands folded the way a person folds their hands. It is
not eating. It is //singing// — a low continuous chitter under the scrabble of the others,
and the longer you listen the less it sounds like vermin-noise and the more it sounds like
//language//, close and wet and old, a thing with grammar in it. The Cantor, some part of you
names it, with no idea where the word came from. It watches you listen, and tips its head, and
you understand it is deciding whether you are far enough gone to be taught.

<<if $magicKnown>>
You carry the Word already; the Cantor hears it in you when you draw near, and goes back to its
singing, having no more to give you that you do not now own.
<<else>>
<<linkreplace "Sit among them and learn the song — let the Cantor teach you the Word">><<nobr>>
<<run setup.learnMagic("cantor")>>
You sit. You let the human part of you that recoils go quiet, and you listen, and you let your
own throat try the shapes — and they //fit//, the way the cheese fit, the way a key you didn't
know you'd been carrying fits a lock you didn't know was yours. The Cantor sings the close wet
syllable and you sing it back, wrong, and again, less wrong, and then right — and something far
down under the floor of the world turns over and //answers//, and the answering pours into you
and waits there now, behind your eyes, a well with a voice. You can speak to the deep. Worse:
it will speak back through whatever you point the Word at — and a little, each time, through you.
//(you have learned the Word — Mana now shows in your kit; in a fight, the Cast action calls it)//
<</nobr>><</linkreplace>>
<</if>>

<<link "Deeper into the nest, where the wheels are cured">><<goto "TheCurdworks">><</link>>
<<link "Back along the culvert, away from the pallet">><<goto "TheCulvertRun">><</link>>


/* ---------- THE CURDWORKS (the rat JOB — cheese-curing; owner 2026-07-20) ----------
   The rat column's JOB, twin of the still and the LAST cell of the food×class matrix. The rats cure the
   fallen pallet (and build more): setup.cheeseShift(mode) — light/hard pay meager coin for rat; the
   standing offer is payment-in-kind — take your wage in the WHEEL (setup.eatCheese, which feeds the
   cheese vice). Spur off TheLarder (the rat FOOD faucet). */

:: TheCurdworks [upper]
Deeper than the pallet, where the heaped wax is oldest and the warm dark is warmest, the bay opens into a
curing-cave the rats have made their whole life's work: rack on rack of wheels going quietly to their
reward, rounds and truckles and great sweating drums of the stuff, some scavenged whole off the pallet and
some — you slowly understand — //built//, pressed up out of God knows what the drains bring down and left
to ripen in the reek. The rat-things tend it with an unhurried craft that is horrible precisely because it
//is// craft: turning the wheels, washing the rinds in a grey trickle you do not look at too closely,
easing back the wax to check the crawling life inside — because the good ones //crawl//, that is the whole
art of it, and the curers are proud of the crawl. Your belly, traitor to the last, waters.

<<if not $curdmasterHeard>>
An old curer straightens off a rack — grey almost all the way over, deft-fingered, patient — and looks you
over the way it looks over a wheel not yet ready.
<<linkreplace "Hear the old curer out">><<nobr>><<set $curdmasterHeard to true>>
//Steady work, this,// it says, the words furred and close. //Turning, washing, tending. Wants doing
right, takes a lifetime to learn doing right, and the pay//— a dry whiskered laugh —//the pay's nothing.
Coin's for topside, for them as still spend it. Down here there's no coin. There's only the //wheel//.// It
taps a ripening round, fond. //So that's how a body's paid here: in what we make. Take your keep in the
cheese and you'll never want, and you'll never leave, and you'll never once mind — I didn't. Started
turning wheels for a night's keep. Look at me now. I //am// the keep. Good, aren't I. Ready right through.//
<</nobr>><</linkreplace>>
<<else>>
The old curer gives you the nod of a thing you tend the racks beside now, and goes back to the turning; the
work does not say it twice.
<</if>>

There is work here for anyone who can stand the reek and hasn't the coin to be proud. What you take for it
is your own affair.

<<linkreplace "Turn the wheels — light work, hands up out of the wash">><<nobr>>
<<set _s to setup.cheeseShift("light")>>
You go down the racks giving the ripening rounds their quarter-turn each, the way you are shown, and it is
honest and it is nothing, and you come away <<if _s.coin gt 0>><<= _s.coin>> coin<<if _s.coin neq 1>>s<</if>> the better<<else>>with a coin that curdles to rubbish before you can pocket it<</if>> and only a little of the cave soaked into you where you weren't watching.
//(<<= _s.rat>> rat worked into you; <<if _s.coin gt 0>>+<<= _s.coin>> coin<<else>>no coin kept<</if>>)//
<</nobr>><</linkreplace>>

<<linkreplace "Wash the rinds — hands deep in the wash, down among the crawling">><<nobr>>
<<set _s to setup.cheeseShift("hard")>>
You work the bad end of it — hands in the grey wash to the wrist, easing wax back off the crawling rounds,
breathing what lifts off them for hours — and it gets into you the way the work always does, under the
nails and into the skin and somewhere behind that, and you come up <<if _s.coin gt 0>><<= _s.coin>> coin<<if _s.coin neq 1>>s<</if>> the better<<else>>with a fist of coin that rots to rubbish before you can spend it<</if>> and wearing more of the cave than you walked in with.
//(<<= _s.rat>> rat worked deep into you; <<if _s.coin gt 0>>+<<= _s.coin>> coin<<else>>no coin kept<</if>>)//
<</nobr>><</linkreplace>>

<<linkreplace "Take your wage in the wheel — coin's for topside">><<nobr>>
<<set _s to setup.cheeseShift("inkind")>>
You work the shift and take your keep the way the curers do — a cured round broken off warm and eaten down
crouched among them, crawling and all, and it is rich and it is animal and it is the //best// part of the
day, and you understand, chewing, exactly why not one of them ever climbed back up the culvert. You pocket
<<if _s.coin gt 0>>a single coin besides<<else>>no coin — there wasn't any, there never is<</if>>, and carry the rest of your wage out inside you.
//(the work: <<= _s.rat>> rat into you. the wage: a whole wheel — +<<= _s.ration.heal>> vigor<<if _s.ration.mana gt 0>>, +<<= _s.ration.mana>> mana<</if>>; <<= _s.ration.bite>> rat worked deeper, and the cheese's hold set <<if setup.dominantAddiction() is "cheese">>hard — it is the loudest thing in you now<<else>>deeper<</if>>)//
<</nobr>><</linkreplace>>

<<link "Back to the pallet">><<goto "TheLarder">><</link>>


:: TheTideFlap [upper]
A tide-gate hangs in the river-wall — a great iron flap, hinged at the top, built to
let the drains empty at low water and clap shut against the river coming back. It is
clapping now, slow and arrhythmic, *boom* and silence and *boom*, as the river
outside breathes against it, and each time it gapes there is a half-second of wider
grey light and colder air and the whole indifferent river-smell before it slams again.

Drawn closer by the light, you see the gate properly: a single slab of riveted iron
the size of a barn door, the hinge at the top weeping orange down a beard of old rust,
the bottom edge worn bright where a hundred years of tide have ground it against the
sill. The river works it from the far side, shouldering it open a hand's breadth and
letting it fall, shouldering and letting fall, never a true rhythm, the muscle of a
whole estuary leaning idly on a door. Cold comes off it in pulses. Each gape throws a
wedge of grey daylight onto the wet floor and snatches it back.

You time it. You cannot help timing it. The gap is a hand's breadth and the iron is
a ton and the river beyond it is January-cold and a mile wide, and it is, you make
yourself admit, not a way out. It is just the closest the drains will let you put
your eye to the open world.

<<if not $timerHeard>>
Crouched by the hinge, timing the boom with a showman's confidence, is a grinning thing that was
once a clever man and is now mostly grin. It has, it wants you to know, the whole business figured.
<<linkreplace "Listen to the grinning thing crouched by the hinge">><<nobr>><<set $timerHeard to true>>
//Don't tell the others,// it says, all teeth, //but I've got this one timed. Boom — and a
three-count — and boom, regular as a clock once you've the ear for it. And on the long gap, the
special one, one rain in twelve, she gapes wide enough for a slim man to be through and into the
river before she claps. I'm a slim man.// It does not move toward the gap. //Stick by me and I'll
wave you through on the same one. Cost you nothing. We'll go together — next big rain. You'll see.//
<</nobr>><</linkreplace>>
<<else>>
It is still timing the flap. Boom, and the three-count, and boom. It tips you a wink that the wide
gap, any rain now, is going to be the one — and lets it clap shut, again, on nothing.
<</if>>

<<link "Back to the overflow chamber">><<goto "TheStormFront">><</link>>
<<link "Toward the barred river-mouth">><<goto "TheRiverMouth">><</link>>


:: TheRainShaft [upper]
It is raining up there, and the rain comes down the shaft — a vertical run to a
roadside gulley far above, and the weather falling through it in a cold clean column,
ringing on the brick, pooling sweet and almost drinkable at your feet.

You crane up into the fall of it. The shaft goes straight and true to a small grey
square of day, and the rain comes down its whole length as a single unbroken
thread, catching what light there is, so that the column seems lit from within — a
rope of falling water you could almost climb with your eyes. It rings on the brick
and beads on your face and runs down your neck, and it is cold the proper way, the
clean way, the cold of weather and not of the deep, and the pool it makes at your
feet is so nearly clear that you could believe, for a breath, the whole place might
one day be rinsed back to honest water.

You stand in the fall of it with your face up and your mouth open and let the
surface rain wash into you, the actual rain, water that was a cloud this morning,
that has touched nothing of the deep, and for as long as you can hold your face to it
you are clean, you are clean, you are being rained on like anyone, like a person
standing at a bus stop in the world.

<<link "Toward a breached subway tunnel">><<goto "TheSubwayBreach">><</link>>
<<link "Back along the culvert">><<goto "TheCulvertRun">><</link>>
<<link "Toward a stair, going up">><<goto "TheStreetStair">><</link>>


:: TheGalleryOfGrates [upper]
A long straight gallery runs under a street, and its whole low roof is grates —
gulley after gulley after pavement-grating, a ladder of grey daylight laid down the
ceiling, and the city walking over all of it. You pass beneath shoes and prams and
a dog that stops and looks down and is hauled on by its lead; beneath conversation,
half-heard, about rent and weather and someone called Denise; beneath the entire
ordinary surface of life, close enough to touch and as reachable as the moon.

The gallery runs on further than you can see, each grate laying its own barred
rectangle of day across the wet floor so the whole length is striped light and dark,
light and dark, like the inside of a thing's ribs. You walk it through alternating
bands — into the day-stripe and the rain spits down on you and the talk overhead
goes suddenly clear, out into the dark between and it all gives way to the drip and
echo of the drain. The surface comes and goes over your head like a signal that
won't quite hold.

And it is never silent here, that is the worst of it: there is always a voice
somewhere down the line, always a footstep on some grate ahead or behind, the city
narrating itself to a listener it does not know it has — a whole warm bright human
murmur, ceaseless, meant for no one, and you walking under all of it in the dark, the
one in the audience.

<<if $corruption gte 70>>
You find you have stopped looking up. The talk of rent and Denise comes down through
the grates like birdsong from a country you've been deported from, and the part of
you that listens does so the way a thing in a hide listens to the field — patiently,
hungrily, with an interest that is no longer homesick and has become something with
more teeth in it.
<<else>>
You walk it slowly, beneath all that careless life, and it is the loneliest stretch
of the whole descent — not the dark places, not the drowned ones, but this: the
world going on a yard above your head, in the light, without you, not even knowing
to miss you.
<</if>>

<<link "Toward a low pavement-grate, and a voice">><<goto "TheAreaGrate">><</link>>
<<link "Toward a stair, going up to a hatch">><<goto "TheStreetStair">><</link>>
<<link "Back to the catch-basin">><<goto "TheCatchbasin">><</link>>


:: TheJunctionVault [upper]
A utility vault where the undergrounds of the city all shake hands: storm-drain and
sewer and the dead bundled cables of telephone and power and the gas main's single
fat artery, all crammed through one brick chamber with the casual danger of work
done by men who are all now dead.

The chamber is low and close and crowded with infrastructure — cable-trays bolted
in tiers up the walls, conduit elbowing past conduit, the gas main running through
the middle of it all at head height so you duck under the city's own breath. Stencils
and tags hang off everything, the freemasonry of trades that never met: a power
crew's hazard diamond, a phone exchange's coded loop, a chalked figure some plumber
left forty years ago and never came back to rub out. It is warm in here, faintly,
and the warmth comes off the live cable somewhere in the bundle — the one dead-looking
thing in the room that is, you realise, still carrying the surface's current right
past you in the dark.

A junction box on the wall still has a faded notice — IN CASE OF FLOODING — and a
number to call, the ink run but legible, a direct line to a surface office that
would, you are fairly sure, still answer, and would not, you are entirely sure,
believe you.

<<if not $inspectorTalked>>
Slumped against the junction box, under its own run-but-legible notice, is the man who came
down to read it: a council inspector dried to leather and half gone to mineral, so much a part
of the brickwork now that you take him for a sandbag until the head turns. The eyes still work.
He knew this whole system once, before it kept him.
<<linkreplace "Ask the dead man what he knows of the way up">><<nobr>><<set $inspectorTalked to true>>
//The rungs,// he says, the voice like a page turning. //You'll have found the shaft off the
outfall. The rungs run out — above head-height they've rusted clean to nothing, and there's no
free-climbing wet brick, not unless you've made your peace with the falling. You want a line. A
rope.// A pause, the patience of a thing with nothing but time. //And there's none up here. Only
place that still deals in rope is back down — through the teeth, in the market. You go down to
come up. That's the joke they built into the plumbing, and nobody left to laugh at it.//
<</nobr>><</linkreplace>>
<<else>>
The inspector has gone back to watching his own notice — IN CASE OF FLOODING, and a number
nobody will answer. He told you the way of it; the brick has the rest of him.
<</if>>

<<link "Through to a breached subway tunnel">><<goto "TheSubwayBreach">><</link>>
<<link "Back to the gauge-house">><<goto "TheGaugeHouse">><</link>>


:: TheStreetStair [upper]
A stair — an actual stair, concrete, with a handrail, going *up* — and at the top of
it a steel hatch with a wheel, the kind of municipal access a worker in a hi-vis
vest comes down through on a Tuesday. You climb it two at a time.

The stair is so ordinary it hurts: poured concrete treads worn to a shine down the
middle, a steel handrail your hand closes round out of plain habit, a painted depth
mark on the wall and a light fitting at the top with the bulb long dead in its cage.
This is a stair built to be walked up and down by people with lanyards and lunchboxes,
a stair that means nothing worse than the end of a shift, and your body climbs it the
way it once climbed a hundred such stairs without a thought, toward the door at the
top and the day on the other side.

You spin the wheel, or you try, and it has not turned in thirty years and does not
turn now, rusted to one solid forged thing, and on the far side of it — you can hear —
is the underside of a manhole cover, and on the far side of *that* is the street, and
the gap between your hands and the open air is four inches of rust and the whole
weight of the world not letting you through.

<<if not setup.has("graft_hand")>>
At the foot of the dead stair, where four inches of rust taunt you with the street, something
crouches that has been working the seam far longer than you. One of its hands has gone over — a
single overgrown claw, levered into the gap, patient and wrong — and it watches you fail at the
wheel with a fellow-feeling worse than mockery. It could give you a hand like its own, it says. A
hand that could force four inches of rust and never feel the splinters. The hatch would stay shut
either way; the deal was never about the door.
<<set _plbl to "Let it do to your hand what the filth did to its (" + setup.tollTag(setup.items.graft_hand.graftSeg, "rat", setup.items.graft_hand.graftAmt) + ", and there is no taking it back)">><<link _plbl>>
<<run setup.installGraft("graft_hand", "rat")>>
<<goto "TheGalleryOfGrates">>
<</link>>
<<link "Refuse — keep the hand you were born with">>
<<soothe 4>>
<<goto "TheGalleryOfGrates">>
<</link>>
<<else>>
The crouched thing has gone back to working its own claw into the seam and does not look up. It
gave you the hand; it has nothing else you would take, and the hatch above is as shut as ever.
<</if>>

<<link "Back down the stair">><<goto "TheGalleryOfGrates">><</link>>
<<link "Toward the rain-shaft">><<goto "TheRainShaft">><</link>>


/* ---------- LAYER-1 BAD END ---------- */

:: EndingHelper [ending]
<<run setup.recordRun("taken")>>
You reach up, because after everything you have refused down here, you cannot refuse
a kind face and a helping hand and the word //home//. The hand closes on your wrist —
warm, strong, ordinary, the most ordinary thing in the world — and it does not pull
you up. It holds you there, at the grate, in the bars of streetlight, while the face
above you keeps its kind shape and lets the eyes finish arriving, a half-beat late,
the way they always finish arriving down here.

//Not yet,// it says, gently, in the warm voice of the world. //You're so nearly
done. Stay where I can find you. We're going to need more of us up here, and you've
come such a long way to be exactly the right kind of thing.//

It lets go. Its feet stand, and ring away across the iron, back into the bright
business of the city it is wearing, and leave you in the falling light four inches
from a surface that is no longer a refuge, because something got there first, and is
keeping your place.

//You reached for the surface, and the surface that reached back had already been
down here.//

<<link "Again">><<run setup.restartCarry()>><</link>>
